**Read:** [Mobility particle size spectrometers — Wiedensohler and colleagues](../sources/Mobility-Particle-Size-Spectrometers-Wiedensohler.lingua#r_1W06WPADV43B98EF8NSW9RDCWM), charge conditioning and multiple-charge inversion; [CPC proportionality calibration — Owen, Mulholland and Guthrie](../sources/CPC-Proportionality-Calibration-Owen-Mulholland-Guthrie.lingua#r_7XKKJ1Q7HNDS2BEPSHZDJMTKVE), aerosol generation and removal of larger particles.

A bipolar charger establishes a distribution of charge states under suitable conditions. Neutral particles remain, and some particles carry more than one elementary charge. The familiar name neutralizer does not mean every outgoing particle has zero charge or exactly one charge.

For fixed selected mobility, a larger multiply charged particle can overlap a smaller singly charged one. In the continuum spherical limit Cc = 1, equal |z|/d gives equal mobility. Thus a 200 nm particle with two charges and a 100 nm particle with one charge would be indistinguishable by mobility alone in that limiting model. At actual nanoscale gas conditions, slip changes the numerical pairing; the ambiguity remains.

The forward response sums charge fractions f_z(d), transmission and detector response across the whole contributing distribution. Unknown particles above a scan's nominal maximum diameter can contribute through multiple charge. An upstream impactor or a justified upper-tail model supplies information, with its own losses and uncertainties.

**Work:** Construct two distributions with the same ideal selected mobility signal but different total particle numbers. Show how an independent upper-size observation or a second charge-sensitive measurement changes the inference. Distinguish a calculated equilibrium charge distribution from evidence that the actual charger, residence time and aerosol attained it.

